<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Upper Palatinate</h1><p class="st-meta">1329 CE – 1628 CE · capital Amberg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Wittelsbach iron country north of the Danube, ruled from Amberg for the Electors Palatine until Bavaria seized it in the Thirty Years' War.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>By the Treaty of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/commune-of-pavia">Pavia</a> in 1329, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-iv-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Louis the Bavarian</a> divided the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-wittelsbach">Wittelsbach</a> possessions, giving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> and the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nordgau">Bavarian Nordgau</a> to his nephews. This upland region between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-regensburg">Regensburg</a> and the Bohemian border, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amberg">Amberg</a>, became known as the Upper Palatinate, an outlying part of the Electoral Palatinate.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amberg">Amberg</a> ran the land for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-elector">electors</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>; from 1595 this was Christian of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-anhalt">Anhalt</a>, architect of Protestant policy. The region's wealth came from iron mining and smelting around Amberg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulzbach-rosenberg">Sulzbach</a>, which made it the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ruhr">Ruhr</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its people were obliged to follow their rulers through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/calvinism">Calvinism</a>, often against the wishes of the estates.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After Elector <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-v-of-the-palatinate">Frederick V</a>'s defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-white-mountain">White Mountain</a> in 1620, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-elector-of-bavaria">Duke Maximilian</a> I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> occupied the Upper <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/electoral-palatinate">Palatinate</a> and in 1628 annexed it, re-Catholicising the population. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-westphalia">Peace of Westphalia</a> confirmed it as part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/upper-palatinate.jpg" alt="Upper Palatinate" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Unknown authorUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flagge_Oberpfalz.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1329 CE – 1628 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Amberg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Frederick V of the Palatinate</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism, Calvinism, then Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bavarian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Oberpfalz</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1478&amp;focus=upper-palatinate" aria-label="Upper Palatinate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4961 459 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Upper Palatinate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5281 715L5200 712M5281 715L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5200 712h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5281 715h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5281 715h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1478&amp;focus=upper-palatinate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
